  "articleBody": "Installing baseboards seems straightforward, but there are a few common pitfalls that can really mess up the final look. One big mistake is measuring everything off-site instead of marking and cutting pieces in place. Walls are rarely perfectly straight or even, so fitting each piece specifically to its spot prevents gaps. Another common issue is using caulk as a substitute for good joinery. Caulk is for sealing small gaps, not for hiding poorly cut or fitted corners. You want crisp, tight joins from the start! Forgetting to fill nail holes and properly sand before painting is another oversight. Those little dimples will show right through the paint if not addressed. And speaking of corners, inside and outside corners that don't meet precisely can make the whole room look a bit off. A consistent reveal and tight contact with the wall are key. To avoid these headaches, make sure you or your installer focuses on precise cuts, takes the time to fill and caulk correctly, and ensures those corners are perfect. If your walls or floors are a bit wonky, or if you're matching complex profiles, a professional touch from someone like HH Remodeling LLC can make all the difference, ensuring a flawless finish.",
